"""English — details, quran (56 questions)."""

D = {
"quran_025": "The surah opens with a question: \"Have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant? Did He not make their plan go astray?\" (105:1-2). The next verses tell how He sent birds in flocks that struck them with stones of baked clay, until they were like straw eaten away (105:3-5). Five verses record one army that came against the House and never reached it. A Muslim learns that no force, however large, can overturn what Allah has decided.",
"quran_026": "The verse reads: \"And your Lord inspired the bee: take for yourself homes among the mountains and the trees and in what they build\" (16:68). The next verse tells the bee to eat of every fruit and follow the ways its Lord made easy, and names its honey a drink of varying colours \"in which there is healing for people\" (16:69). The bee follows a guidance it was given. A Muslim learns to see the work of a small creature as a sign pointing to its Creator.",
"quran_027": "The Qur'an says: \"Until, when they came upon the valley of the ants, an ant said: O ants, enter your dwellings, lest Sulayman and his soldiers crush you while they do not notice\" (27:18). Sulayman smiled at her words and prayed: \"My Lord, enable me to be grateful for Your favour which You have bestowed upon me and upon my parents\" (27:19). A tiny creature caring for its own moved a king to thanks rather than pride. A Muslim learns that every blessing calls for gratitude.",
"quran_028": "The verse gives the full comparison: \"The likeness of those who take protectors other than Allah is the likeness of the spider that takes a house; and indeed the frailest of houses is the spider's house, if only they knew\" (29:41). Allah then says that \"these examples We present to the people, but none will understand them except those of knowledge\" (29:43). A web looks like shelter and gives none against the lightest touch. A Muslim learns to put his trust in Allah alone, whose protection does not give way.",
"quran_029": "The story begins: \"And when Musa said to his people: Allah commands you to slaughter a cow, they said: Do you take us in ridicule?\" (2:67). They asked what kind of cow it was, then its colour, then its description again, and each answer narrowed the choice: neither old nor young, bright yellow, unyoked and without blemish (2:68-71). \"Then they slaughtered it, and they were near to not doing it\" (2:71). A Muslim learns to meet a command with willing obedience, not with questions that make it harder.",
"quran_030": "The verse describes the pagans' practice: \"And they assign to Allah, from what He created of crops and livestock, a share, and they say: This is for Allah, by their claim, and this is for our partners\" (6:136). The surah then rejects the cattle they declared forbidden and asks about the eight pairs of sheep, goats, camels and cattle: \"Say: Is it the two males He has forbidden, or the two females?\" (6:143). A Muslim learns that only Allah decides what is lawful and what is not.",
"quran_031": "The refrain reads: \"So which of the favours of your Lord will you deny?\" (55:13). It first appears after the earth is described as laid out for all creatures, with fruit, date-palms, grain and fragrant plants (55:10-12), and returns after the creation of man, the two seas, the ships like mountains, and the Gardens promised to those who fear their Lord. The address is to two, mankind and jinn, called together in 55:33. A Muslim learns to answer each repetition by counting a favour and refusing to deny it.",
"quran_032": "The verse states: \"And they remained in their cave for three hundred years, and exceeded by nine\" (18:25). At once Allah tells the Prophet ﷺ: \"Say: Allah is most knowing of how long they remained\" (18:26). When the sleepers woke, one had guessed, \"We have remained a day or part of a day\" (18:19). The exact figure comes from Allah, not from them. A Muslim learns that knowledge of hidden matters is Allah's, and His word on them is final.",
"quran_033": "The verse reads: \"And you would think them awake, while they were asleep; and We turned them to the right and to the left, while their dog stretched its forelegs at the entrance\" (18:18). The sleepers were young men who believed in their Lord (18:13) and fled to the cave for their faith (18:16). Allah turned their bodies as they slept while the dog kept the entrance. A Muslim learns that Allah's care reaches those who leave everything for their faith, down to the animal beside them.",
"quran_034": "The people said: \"O Dhul-Qarnayn, indeed Ya'juj and Ma'juj are causing corruption in the land, so may we assign for you a payment, that you might make between us and them a barrier?\" (18:94). He answered that what his Lord had given him was better, and had iron piled between the mountain sides and molten copper poured over it (18:95-96). Then he said: \"This is a mercy from my Lord\" (18:98). A Muslim learns to use power as a trust and credit the result to Allah.",
"quran_035": "The verse reads: \"Allah is the Light of the heavens and the earth. The likeness of His light is a niche in which is a lamp … lit from a blessed olive tree. Light upon light. Allah guides to His light whom He wills\" (24:35). The next verse places that light \"in houses which Allah has permitted to be raised and His name to be mentioned in them\" (24:36). A Muslim learns that guidance is a light Allah gives.",
"quran_036": "The hadith is narrated by Abu Sa'id al-Khudri in Sahih al-Bukhari 5013. A man heard another reciting \"Qul huwa Allahu ahad\"; in the morning he told the Prophet ﷺ, who swore by Him in whose hand is his soul that it equals a third of the Qur'an. The surah is four verses: \"Say: He is Allah, One; Allah, the Eternal Refuge; He neither begets nor is born; and there is none comparable to Him\" (112:1-4). A Muslim learns that a few words about Allah carry great weight.",
"quran_037": "The hadith is narrated by Ubayy ibn Ka'b in Sahih Muslim 810. The Prophet ﷺ asked him, \"O Abu al-Mundhir, do you know which verse of the Book of Allah with you is greatest?\" Ubayy answered with the verse opening \"Allah, there is no god but He, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer\" (2:255), and the Prophet ﷺ struck his chest and said, \"May knowledge be a delight to you, Abu al-Mundhir.\" The verse continues that His Kursi extends over the heavens and the earth. A Muslim learns Ayat al-Kursi by heart.",
"quran_038": "The surah begins: \"May the hands of Abu Lahab perish, and may he perish. His wealth will not avail him, nor what he earned\" (111:1-2). It continues that he will enter a fire of flame, and with him his wife, the carrier of firewood (111:3-5). He is the only opponent of the Prophet ﷺ whom the Qur'an names, and his end was announced while he lived to hear it. A Muslim learns that neither kinship to the Prophet ﷺ nor wealth saves one who rejects the message.",
"quran_039": "The surah closes: \"To you your religion, and to me my religion\" (109:6). It opens with a command to the Prophet ﷺ, \"Say: O disbelievers, I do not worship what you worship, nor are you worshippers of what I worship\" (109:1-3), and the two refusals are then repeated (109:4-5). The closing verse is not a threat but a plain statement: the worship of Allah alone and the worship of idols cannot be mixed or traded. A Muslim learns to hold to his own worship without compromise and without hostility.",
"quran_040": "The surah reads in full: \"When the help of Allah and the conquest come, and you see the people entering the religion of Allah in crowds, then glorify the praise of your Lord and ask His forgiveness; indeed, He is ever accepting of repentance\" (110:1-3). It describes complete success, and the command that follows is not celebration but praise and asking pardon. The words are addressed to the Prophet ﷺ himself at the height of his mission. A Muslim learns that the right answer to victory is thanks and humility.",
"quran_041": "The surah reads: \"By time! Indeed, mankind is in loss, except those who believe and do righteous deeds and urge one another to the truth and urge one another to patience\" (103:1-3). Allah swears by time itself, then states that everyone is losing except one group. The exception has four parts, two concerning the person, faith and good deeds, and two concerning others, reminding them of the truth and encouraging them to hold on. A Muslim learns that being saved includes helping others stay on the path.",
"quran_042": "The surah opens: \"Have you seen the one who denies the Recompense? That is the one who drives away the orphan and does not urge the feeding of the poor\" (107:1-3). It then turns to those who pray: \"woe to those who pray, who are heedless of their prayer, who make a show, and withhold small kindnesses\" (107:4-7). The title Al-Ma'un is that final word, the everyday help one neighbour gives another. A Muslim learns that belief in the Judgement shows in how the orphan and the poor are treated.",
"quran_043": "The first revelation reads: \"Read, in the name of your Lord who created\" (96:1), \"who created man from a clinging clot\" (96:2), and continues: \"Read, and your Lord is the most Generous, who taught by the pen, taught man what he did not know\" (96:3-5). Man's small beginning is set beside Allah's generosity in teaching him. The surah then warns that man oversteps \"because he sees himself self-sufficient\" (96:6-7). A Muslim learns that knowledge begins in Allah's name, and that remembering one's origin keeps pride away.",
"quran_044": "The verse reads: \"And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated, and the great Qur'an\" (15:87). The next verse tells the Prophet ﷺ not to look longingly at what the disbelievers were given to enjoy (15:88): what he holds is worth more. Al-Fatiha has seven verses, opening with \"All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (1:2) and ending with the request for the straight path (1:6-7). A Muslim learns to treasure the short surah he repeats in every prayer as one of his greatest gifts.",
"quran_045": "The surah opens: \"Blessed is He who sent down the Criterion upon His servant, that he may be to the worlds a warner\" (25:1). The next verse names the Sender: He to whom belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, who has taken no son and has no partner (25:2). The Qur'an gives the same name to what Musa and Harun received: \"We gave Musa and Harun the Furqan\" (21:48). A Muslim learns to read the Qur'an as the measure by which he tells truth from falsehood.",
"quran_046": "The surah opens with a chain of oaths: \"By the Mount, and by a Book inscribed on an unrolled parchment, and by the frequented House\" (52:1-4), leading to the statement, \"indeed, the punishment of your Lord will occur\" (52:7). The Qur'an recalls the Mount as the place where Allah called Musa \"from the right side of the Mount and brought him near, conversing with him\" (19:52). A Muslim learns that the Day of Judgement is spoken of with the same certainty as a mountain.",
"quran_047": "The surah opens: \"By the star when it descends, your companion has not strayed, nor has he erred, nor does he speak from his own desire; it is only a revelation revealed\" (53:1-4). The oath by the star introduces a defence of the Prophet ﷺ: his words are not his own invention. The surah then tells of the one \"mighty in power\" who taught him (53:5-10). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an came to the Prophet ﷺ from outside himself, revealed and not composed.",
"quran_048": "The surah reads: \"Indeed, We sent it down on the Night of Decree … The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months\" (97:1-3), and then: \"The angels and the Spirit descend in it, by permission of their Lord, for every matter. Peace it is until the emergence of dawn\" (97:4-5). What is sent down is the Qur'an, which the Qur'an places in Ramadan (2:185). A Muslim learns to seek that night in Ramadan and spend it in worship.",
"quran_049": "The verse reads: \"And We send down of the Qur'an that which is healing and mercy for the believers, but it does not increase the wrongdoers except in loss\" (17:82). The same words heal the believer and add loss to the one who rejects them. Earlier in the surah the Qur'an is described as guiding \"to that which is most upright\" (17:9). A Muslim learns to come to the Qur'an as a patient comes to a remedy, trusting it and applying it.",
"quran_050": "The verse reads: \"And if you are in doubt about what We have sent down upon Our servant, then produce a surah the like of it\" (2:23). The next verse adds: \"But if you do not, and you will never be able to, then fear the Fire\" (2:24). Elsewhere the Qur'an states that if all mankind and jinn gathered, \"they could not produce the like of it\" (17:88). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an's proof is itself: open to every challenger and never matched.",
"quran_051": "The hadith is narrated by Abu Hurayra in Sahih al-Bukhari 4704: the Prophet ﷺ said that Umm al-Qur'an is the seven oft-repeated verses and the great Qur'an. The words echo the verse \"We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated, and the great Qur'an\" (15:87), fixing its meaning on Al-Fatiha. The surah holds praise of Allah, \"You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help\" (1:5), and the plea for guidance. A Muslim learns why the surah opening every prayer is called its Mother.",
"quran_052": "The label is written under the name of each surah in every printed mushaf: Al-Fatiha is Makki, Al-Baqara is Madani, and so on. The Hijra that divides the two periods is recalled in the Qur'an itself: when the disbelievers drove the Prophet ﷺ out, he was \"one of two, when they were in the cave\" (9:40). The Qur'an also names the two groups the migration created, the Muhajirun and the Ansar (9:100). A Muslim learns to read a surah knowing its period, to understand whom it first addressed.",
"quran_053": "Al-Baqara begins: \"Alif, Lam, Mim. This is the Book about which there is no doubt, a guidance for the God-fearing\" (2:1-2), and Al 'Imran begins: \"Alif, Lam, Mim. Allah, there is no god but He, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer\" (3:1-2). Other surahs add a letter to the same three, Alif Lam Mim Sad in 7:1 and Alif Lam Mim Ra in 13:1, and are not counted among the six. A Muslim learns to recite these openings letter by letter, as they are written, as a mark shared by these surahs.",
"quran_054": "The letters come in every length. A single letter opens Sad (38:1), Qaf (50:1) and Al-Qalam, \"Nun. By the pen and what they inscribe\" (68:1); five letters open Maryam, \"Kaf, Ha, Ya, 'Ayn, Sad\" (19:1). They are recited by the names of the letters, not joined into a word, and are often followed at once by the Book itself: \"Ya-Sin. By the wise Qur'an\" (36:1-2). A Muslim reads them as written, certain that the whole Qur'an, including what he does not grasp, is from Allah.",
"quran_055": "The verse reads: \"And those who believe and do righteous deeds and believe in what has been sent down upon Muhammad, He will remove from them their misdeeds and amend their condition\" (47:2). It follows a verse about those who disbelieve, whose deeds He will waste (47:1). The Prophet's ﷺ name appears in the Qur'an four times in all: here and in 3:144, 33:40 and 48:29. A Muslim learns that belief in what was sent down upon Muhammad ﷺ is how one's condition is set right.",
"quran_056": "The verse reads: \"And when Luqman said to his son, while instructing him: O my son, do not associate anything with Allah; indeed, association is a great injustice\" (31:13). Just before, Allah says He gave Luqman wisdom (31:12). The advice continues: establish the prayer, enjoin what is right, be patient (31:17); do not turn your cheek away from people in contempt (31:18); be moderate in your pace and lower your voice (31:19). A Muslim learns that the first lesson to give a child is the oneness of Allah.",
"quran_057": "The surah opens without the basmala, alone among the 114, with the words: \"A disavowal from Allah and His Messenger to those with whom you had made a treaty among the idolaters\" (9:1). Those who kept their treaty are excepted at once (9:4). The name At-Tawba comes from later in the surah, where Allah turns in mercy to the Prophet ﷺ, the Muhajirun, the Ansar and the three who were left behind (9:117-118). A Muslim learns that the surah of disavowal is also the surah of repentance accepted.",
"quran_058": "The surah opens: \"Ya-Sin. By the wise Qur'an, indeed you are among the messengers, on a straight path\" (36:1-4). The oath by the Qur'an is followed by its purpose, \"that you may warn a people whose forefathers were not warned\" (36:6). The surah then tells of a town that received messengers and of the man who came running from its farthest end to urge his people to follow them (36:13-20). A Muslim learns from its opening that the Prophet's ﷺ mission rests on the Qur'an itself.",
"quran_059": "The surah opens: \"Blessed is He in whose hand is the dominion, and He is over all things competent, who created death and life to test you as to which of you is best in deed\" (67:1-2). The One who holds all sovereignty set the test, and it is measured in deeds. It then points to the seven heavens: \"do you see any breaks?\" (67:3). A Muslim learns that his whole life is lived inside Allah's kingdom, where deeds are what count.",
"quran_060": "The surah reads in full: \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind, the King of mankind, the God of mankind, from the evil of the retreating whisperer, who whispers in the breasts of mankind, from among jinn and men\" (114:1-6). Three names of the Lord are set against one enemy, the whisperer who draws back. The Qur'an thus opens with Al-Fatiha, a prayer for guidance, and closes with a prayer for protection. A Muslim learns to end his reading, as the Book itself ends, in Allah's keeping.",
"quran_061": "The surah reads: \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the daybreak, from the evil of what He created\" (113:1-2), and then from darkness, from those who blow on knots, and from the envier (113:3-5). Four evils are named, and refuge from all of them is sought in the One who splits the night with dawn. Its companion, An-Nas, names one evil, the whisperer (114:4). A Muslim learns to name what he fears and to turn from it to the Lord who brings light out of darkness.",
"quran_062": "The verse reads: \"Indeed, as-Safa and al-Marwa are among the symbols of Allah. So whoever makes pilgrimage to the House or performs 'Umrah, there is no blame upon him for walking between them. And whoever volunteers good, then indeed Allah is Appreciative and Knowing\" (2:158). The same surah recalls Ibrahim and Isma'il raising the foundations of the House (2:127), so the two hills belong to the story of the House beside them. A Muslim learns that the places of worship in Makkah are Allah's signs, walked with reverence.",
"quran_063": "The surah says: \"And mention in the Book Maryam, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east\" (19:16). She placed a screen between herself and them, and Allah sent to her His Spirit, who appeared as a well-formed man; she said, \"I seek refuge in the Most Merciful from you, if you fear Allah\", and he told her he was sent to give her a pure boy (19:17-19). A Muslim learns from her modesty and her immediate turning to Allah when afraid.",
"quran_064": "The verse recalls the Prophet ﷺ saying to Zayd, \"Keep your wife and fear Allah\", and continues: \"So when Zayd had ended his relationship with her, We married her to you\" (33:37). The same surah had declared, \"He has not made your adopted sons your sons\" (33:4), and commanded, \"Call them by their fathers\" (33:5). A Muslim learns that an adopted child is loved and cared for while keeping the name of his own father.",
"quran_065": "The verse reads: \"Sulayman did not disbelieve, but the devils disbelieved, teaching people magic and that which was sent down upon the two angels at Babylon, Harut and Marut. But the two did not teach anyone until they had said: We are only a trial, so do not disbelieve\" (2:102). The verse adds that whoever bought this had no share in the Hereafter. A Muslim learns that magic is a trial to be refused, and that Sulayman is cleared of any part in it.",
"quran_066": "The verse reads: \"O mankind, indeed We have created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another. Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allah is the most God-fearing of you\" (49:13). It comes after verses forbidding believers to ridicule one another, to use insulting nicknames, to suspect, spy or backbite (49:11-12). Peoples and tribes exist for acquaintance, not for ranking. A Muslim learns to look for worth in a person's piety and not in lineage or origin.",
"quran_067": "The surah opens: \"When the Event befalls, there is, at its befalling, no denial. It brings down some and raises others. When the earth is shaken with a shaking and the mountains are crumbled to crumbling\" (56:1-5). Then it divides all people into three: \"the companions of the right\", \"the companions of the left\", and \"the forerunners, the forerunners; those are the ones brought near\" (56:7-11). A Muslim learns to ask which of the three he is preparing to stand among.",
"quran_068": "The surah opens: \"Nun. By the pen and what they inscribe, you are not, by the favour of your Lord, a madman. And indeed, you are of a great moral character\" (68:1-4). Allah swears by the pen and the written line to defend His Prophet ﷺ against the charge his people threw at him. The pen is also named in the first revelation, where Allah is He \"who taught by the pen\" (96:4). A Muslim learns that writing and knowledge are honoured by Allah's own oath.",
"quran_069": "The surah opens with oaths: \"By the dawn, and by the ten nights, and by the even and the odd, and by the night when it passes; is there in that an oath for one of reason?\" (89:1-5). Then it recalls what became of those who defied their Lord: 'Ad, Thamud who carved rock in the valley, and Pharaoh of the stakes, on whom \"your Lord poured a scourge of punishment\" (89:6-13). A Muslim learns that Allah watches every people and every age.",
"quran_070": "The surah opens with oaths: \"By the sun and its brightness, and the moon when it follows it … and the sky and He who built it, and the earth and He who spread it, and the soul and He who proportioned it\" (91:1-2, 91:5-7). All of them lead to one sentence: \"He has succeeded who purifies it, and he has failed who buries it\" (91:9-10). A Muslim learns that the sun and the soul are both Allah's work, and his task is the cleansing of the second.",
"quran_071": "The surah opens: \"Has there come upon man a period of time when he was not a thing mentioned? Indeed, We created man from a drop of mixed fluid, to test him\" (76:1-2). It then describes the righteous who give food, despite their love for it, to the needy, the orphan and the captive, \"only for the face of Allah\" (76:8-9). A Muslim learns to remember that he was once nothing, and to answer that gift with gratitude and giving.",
"quran_072": "The verse reads: \"And We have certainly made the Qur'an easy for remembrance, so is there any who will remember?\" (54:17). It comes right after the story of Nuh, whose people called him a liar until the gates of the sky were opened with water (54:9-16), and returns after the stories of 'Ad, Thamud and Lut (54:22, 54:32, 54:40). The ease belongs to the Book; the remembering is asked of the reader. A Muslim learns that memorising the Qur'an is within reach of anyone who turns to it.",
"quran_073": "The surah opens: \"The Hour has come near, and the moon has split. And if they see a sign, they turn away and say: Passing magic\" (54:1-2). The next verse adds that they denied and followed their own desires (54:3). A sign was shown in the sky itself, and those who saw it called it magic and moved on. The surah then goes through the peoples who denied before them, Nuh's, 'Ad, Thamud, Lut's and Pharaoh's. A Muslim learns that a sign convinces only a heart willing to be convinced.",
"quran_074": "The verse answers an objection: \"And those who disbelieve say: Why was the Qur'an not sent down to him all at once? Thus, that We may strengthen your heart thereby, and We have recited it in slow, distinct recitation\" (25:32). The Qur'an says the same elsewhere: \"a Qur'an which We have divided, that you might recite it to the people over a prolonged period\" (17:106). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an is best received as it came, little by little.",
"quran_075": "The hadith is narrated by 'A'isha in Sahih al-Bukhari 5017. Every night at bedtime the Prophet ﷺ joined his palms, blew into them and recited \"Qul huwa Allahu ahad\", \"Qul a'udhu bi-rabbi-l-falaq\" and \"Qul a'udhu bi-rabbi-n-nas\"; then he wiped with them whatever he could reach of his body, beginning with his head and face, three times. The three surahs declare Allah's oneness and ask His refuge (112:1, 113:1, 114:1). A Muslim learns a simple habit for the last minutes of the day, done as the Prophet ﷺ did.",
"quran_076": "The last verse of Al-'Alaq reads: \"No! Do not obey him. But prostrate and draw near\" (96:19), spoken after the warning against the one who forbids a servant when he prays (96:9-10). The Qur'an describes the believers' response to such verses: \"when the verses of the Most Merciful were recited to them, they fell in prostration and weeping\" (19:58). Every printed mushaf marks these verses with a sign in the margin. A Muslim who reads or hears one of them prostrates, joining word and act as the verse asks.",
"quran_077": "The word tajwid means making something good and beautiful, and the science takes its charge from the Qur'an: \"and recite the Qur'an with measured recitation\" (73:4), and \"We have recited it in slow, distinct recitation\" (25:32). Its rules cover where each letter is formed in the mouth and throat, how long a vowel is held, when a sound is nasalised. A learner acquires it by hearing a teacher and repeating. A Muslim learns that reciting the Qur'an correctly is itself a form of care for the Book.",
"quran_078": "The title hafiz comes from the same root as Allah's promise about the Book: \"Indeed, it is We who sent down the Reminder, and indeed We are its guardians\" (15:9). The Qur'an describes itself as \"clear verses within the breasts of those who have been given knowledge\" (29:49), and as \"easy for remembrance\" (54:17). A hafiz has learned all 114 surahs from Al-Fatiha to An-Nas, and keeps them by regular recitation. A Muslim honours the hafiz because the Qur'an lives in his memory as it did in the first companions.",
"quran_079": "The word mushaf comes from sahifa, a written page, and the Qur'an speaks of its revelation as held \"in honoured sheets, exalted and purified\" (80:13-14). A mushaf holds the 114 surahs in their fixed order, from Al-Fatiha to An-Nas, with the name of each surah and its period, Makki or Madani, written above it. Each juz' is further divided into two ahzab, marked in the margin. A Muslim treats the mushaf with respect and reads from it regularly, knowing it is the Book Allah has promised to guard (15:9).",
"quran_080": "The queen read the letter to her council: \"Indeed, it is from Sulayman, and indeed it is: In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful. Do not be haughty with me, but come to me in submission\" (27:30-31). The hoopoe had brought Sulayman news of a queen whose people prostrated to the sun (27:22-24), and Sulayman sent the letter with him (27:28). The queen called it \"a noble letter\" (27:29), opened with the basmala. A Muslim learns to begin what he writes in the name of Allah.",
}
